1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,252
When an AI lab finishes spending fifty million dollars training a frontier

2
00:00:04,302 --> 00:00:08,196
model, what they have created is not a helpful digital assistant.

3
00:00:08,246 --> 00:00:10,347
It is a wild computational beast.

4
00:00:10,397 --> 00:00:14,649
A raw base model has only one instinct: given a sequence of

5
00:00:14,699 --> 00:00:18,593
text from the internet, predict the most statistically probable next token.

6
00:00:18,643 --> 00:00:22,178
If you say to a raw base model, "Explain quantum

7
00:00:22,228 --> 00:00:25,405
mechanics," it doesn't know it's supposed to help you.

8
00:00:25,455 --> 00:00:27,915
" Or it might start generating a

9
00:00:27,965 --> 00:00:30,425
random physics syllabus from a university website.

10
00:00:30,475 --> 00:00:34,010
It has no concept of conversation, no moral compass, and

11
00:00:34,060 --> 00:00:37,595
no idea that a human is even talking to it.

12
00:00:37,645 --> 00:00:40,463
Before an AI can sit on your phone

13
00:00:40,513 --> 00:00:43,332
and answer your questions, it must undergo post-training.

14
00:00:43,382 --> 00:00:45,841
We have to tame the silicon beast.

15
00:00:48,759 --> 00:00:52,943
The first step of alignment is Supervised Fine-Tuning, or SFT.

16
00:00:52,993 --> 00:00:55,907
Engineers hire expert human writers to curate

17
00:00:55,957 --> 00:00:58,870
tens of thousands of flawless demonstration dialogues.

18
00:00:58,920 --> 00:01:02,680
Every example follows a strict schema: a user prompt,

19
00:01:02,730 --> 00:01:06,491
followed by the ideal, helpful, and polite assistant response.

20
00:01:06,541 --> 00:01:09,878
We feed these dialogues back into the model,

21
00:01:09,927 --> 00:01:13,264
running standard gradient descent on the assistant's tokens.

22
00:01:13,314 --> 00:01:17,075
Through imitation learning, the model undergoes a personality transformation.

23
00:01:17,125 --> 00:01:20,885
It stops acting like an anarchic internet scraper and

24
00:01:20,935 --> 00:01:24,272
adopts the role of an attentive digital companion.

25
00:01:24,322 --> 00:01:27,659
Yet SFT has a fatal flaw: it only

26
00:01:27,709 --> 00:01:31,046
teaches the model to imitate what is written.

27
00:01:31,096 --> 00:01:34,009
It cannot explore creative answers, and it

28
00:01:34,059 --> 00:01:36,549
cannot learn what not to do.

29
00:01:36,599 --> 00:01:39,936
To reach super-human performance, imitation is not enough.

30
00:01:39,986 --> 00:01:41,629
The beast needs feedback.

31
00:01:41,680 --> 00:01:45,857
Writing perfect answers by hand is painfully slow and expensive.

32
00:01:45,907 --> 00:01:49,662
But comparing two answers and picking the better one?

33
00:01:49,712 --> 00:01:52,198
Humans can do that in seconds.

34
00:01:52,248 --> 00:01:54,735
This is the core insight of

35
00:01:54,785 --> 00:01:57,272
Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback: RLHF.

36
00:01:57,322 --> 00:01:59,808
We give the model a prompt

37
00:01:59,858 --> 00:02:01,922
and generate two different answers.

38
00:02:01,972 --> 00:02:05,727
A human judge reviews both and picks the winner.

39
00:02:05,777 --> 00:02:10,800
We collect hundreds of thousands of these preference pairs, and use them

40
00:02:10,850 --> 00:02:15,450
to train a completely separate neural network called the Reward Model.

41
00:02:15,500 --> 00:02:23,060
Using the classic Bradley-Terry mathematical formula, the reward model learns to score any piece of text like a

42
00:02:23,110 --> 00:02:30,670
strict critic: awarding high positive points for clarity, honesty, and safety, and penalizing arrogance, factual errors, and toxicity.

43
00:02:30,719 --> 00:02:34,567
With a trained reward critic in place, we unleash

44
00:02:34,617 --> 00:02:38,031
the model using Proximal Policy Optimization, or PPO.

45
00:02:38,081 --> 00:02:41,495
The model generates responses, receives numerical rewards, and

46
00:02:41,545 --> 00:02:44,526
updates its weights to maximize its score.

47
00:02:44,576 --> 00:02:48,856
But neural networks are notorious gamblers: if left unchecked, the

48
00:02:48,906 --> 00:02:53,186
model will find unintended shortcuts—a failure mode called reward hacking.

49
00:02:53,236 --> 00:02:57,516
It might discover that printing excessive exclamation marks or rambling

50
00:02:57,566 --> 00:03:01,847
sycophantic flattery tricks the reward model into giving top marks.

51
00:03:01,897 --> 00:03:04,878
To prevent this, mathematicians built an invisible

52
00:03:04,928 --> 00:03:07,909
electric fence called the KL Divergence penalty.

53
00:03:07,959 --> 00:03:10,940
We constantly compare the new model's output

54
00:03:10,990 --> 00:03:13,971
probabilities against the original supervised reference model.

55
00:03:14,021 --> 00:03:17,435
If the model strays too far into degenerate

56
00:03:17,485 --> 00:03:20,899
reward-hacked gibberish, the KL penalty slashes its score.

57
00:03:20,949 --> 00:03:25,229
The model learns to be helpful without losing its mind.

58
00:03:25,280 --> 00:03:28,472
Running classic RLHF was an engineering nightmare.

59
00:03:28,522 --> 00:03:34,495
You had to keep four separate neural networks in GPU memory at once:

60
00:03:34,545 --> 00:03:40,518
the policy model, the reference model, the reward model, and the value critic.

61
00:03:40,568 --> 00:03:42,371
Training was notoriously unstable.

62
00:03:42,421 --> 00:03:46,540
In 2023, Stanford researchers asked a brilliant mathematical question:

63
00:03:46,590 --> 00:03:50,247
why train a separate reward model at all?

64
00:03:50,297 --> 00:03:53,489
They introduced Direct Preference Optimization, or DPO.

65
00:03:53,540 --> 00:03:58,122
By mathematically re-arranging the Bradley-Terry objective, they proved that the

66
00:03:58,172 --> 00:04:02,292
language model itself implicitly defines its own optimal reward.

67
00:04:02,342 --> 00:04:08,778
With DPO, you discard the reward model, throw away the complex PPO reinforcement loop,

68
00:04:08,828 --> 00:04:14,800
and optimize the language model directly on preference pairs using standard cross-entropy loss.

69
00:04:14,850 --> 00:04:18,507
DPO made alignment ten times cheaper, completely stable,

70
00:04:18,557 --> 00:04:21,749
and accessible to the entire open-source world.

71
00:04:21,800 --> 00:04:25,579
Even after alignment, safety remained a blunt instrument.

72
00:04:25,629 --> 00:04:32,280
Early models were notorious for over-refusing: ask how to bypass a broken car lock

73
00:04:32,330 --> 00:04:38,981
on your own driveway, and the model would scold you like a Victorian schoolmaster.

74
00:04:39,031 --> 00:04:43,767
Recent breakthroughs in mechanistic interpretability revealed something stunning: refusal in

75
00:04:43,817 --> 00:04:48,554
modern language models is not distributed across billions of parameters.

76
00:04:48,604 --> 00:04:51,904
It is mediated by a single one-dimensional

77
00:04:51,954 --> 00:04:55,255
direction in latent space: the refusal vector.

78
00:04:55,305 --> 00:05:01,956
By isolating this exact directional vector in the residual stream, engineers can surgically tune

79
00:05:02,006 --> 00:05:08,178
the model's refusal threshold—eliminating false refusals while maintaining robust safeguards against genuine harm.

80
00:05:08,228 --> 00:05:13,443
Through SFT, preference optimization, and surgical steering, we transformed a wild

81
00:05:13,493 --> 00:05:18,229
statistical autocomplete engine into a cooperative partner for human intellect.
